<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?><library hasTree="0" revision="R2024b"><block_node SID="121" hasTree="0"><block_name>EVM Measurement</block_name><block_class>EVM Measurement</block_class><block_desc>Calculate the RMS Error Vector Magnitude (EVM) between a reference signal and a corrupted received signal of an input frame. The RMS EVM measurement is defined in different standards as the (%) ratio of the RMS error to the square root of either one of the following:
1) Average reference signal power
2) Average constellation power
3) Peak constellation power

All power values are given in Watts, referenced to 1 ohm.

The optional maximum EVM is the maximum EVM value in an input frame.The optional X-percentile output is the EVM value below which X% of the computed EVM values lie.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/EVM Measurement</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/EVM Measurement</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>35   69  110  116</block_position><block_icon>EVM@20@Measurement.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:2,signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>MATLABSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="122" hasTree="0"><block_name>MER Measurement</block_name><block_class>MER Measurement</block_class><block_desc>Calculate the Modulation Error Ratio (MER) between a reference signal and a corrupted received signal of an input frame. The MER measurement is defined as 10*log(average reference signal power/mean square error) in an input frame. 

The optional minimum MER output is the minimum MER value in an input frame.

The optional X-percentile output is the MER value above which X% of the computed MER values lie.</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/MER Measurement</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/MER Measurement</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>155   68  230  112</block_position><block_icon>MER@20@Measurement.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:2,signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>MATLABSystem</block_type><block_reference></block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="27" hasTree="0"><block_name>dB Conversion</block_name><block_class>dB Conversion</block_class><block_desc>dsp:DSPMatrixOperationsBlockDialog:dBConversion_MaskDesc</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/dB Conversion</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/dB Conversion</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>275   70  365  120</block_position><block_icon>dB@20@Conversion.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1,signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>SubSystem</block_type><block_reference>dspmathops/dB Conversion</block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="125" hasTree="0"><block_name>Power Meter</block_name><block_class>dsp.simulink.PowerMeter</block_class><block_desc>dsp:system:powermeter:BlockHeader</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Power Meter</block_path><block_open>$bdroot/Power Meter</block_open><block_isNotLeafBlk>0</block_isNotLeafBlk><block_position>410   70  500  120</block_position><block_icon>Power@20@Meter.svg</block_icon><keywords></keywords><port_domains>signal.in_signal:1,signal.out_signal:1</port_domains><block_type>MATLABSystem</block_type><block_reference>dspstat3/Power Meter</block_reference></block_node><block_node SID="126" hasTree="0"><block_name>Integer to Bit\nConverter</block_name><block_class>Integer to Bit Converter</block_class><block_desc>Simulink:blocks:IntegerToBitConverter</block_desc><block_path>$bdroot/Integer to Bit\nConverter</block_path><block_open>$bdr